.TH "TEXTTRACK.CUECHANGE_EVENT" "3JS" "October 27, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
TextTrack.cuechange_event \- TextTrack: cuechange event
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]cuechange\f[B]\f[R] event fires when a
\f[CR]TextTrack\f[R] has changed the currently displaying cues.
The event is fired on both the \f[CR]TextTrack\f[R] and the
\f[CR]HTMLTrackElement\f[R] in which it\(cqs being presented, if any.
.SH SYNTAX
Use the event name in \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
addEventListener(\(dqcuechange\(dq, (event) => { })
.EE
.PP
Alternatively, assign to the event handler property of the interface:
.IP
.EX
oncuechange = (event) => { }
.EE
.SH EVENT TYPE
A generic \f[CR]Event\f[R] with no added properties.
.SH EXAMPLES
You can set up a listener for the \f[CR]cuechange\f[R] event on a
\f[CR]TextTrack\f[R] using the \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R] method:
.IP
.EX
track.addEventListener(\(dqcuechange\(dq, () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]const\f[R] cues = track.activeCues; \f[I]// array of current cues\f[R]
  \f[I]// \&...\f[R]
});
.EE
.PP
Or you can set the \f[CR]oncuechange\f[R] event handler property (but
note that this replaces other event listeners added using
\f[CR]oncuechange\f[R], if any):
.IP
.EX
track.oncuechange = (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]let\f[R] cues = track.activeCues; \f[I]// array of current cues\f[R]
};
.EE
